---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import site from '../../site.config.json' with { type: 'json' };

const page = {
  url: '/about/', title: 'About Marine Diesel Engine Australia',
  seoTitle: 'About Marine Diesel Engine Australia', pageType: 'about',
  status: 'ready', author: site.defaultAuthor, reviewer: null,
  needsReviewer: false, parent: '/', parentTitle: 'Home',
  schema: ['AboutPage', 'BreadcrumbList'], updated: '2026-10-02',
  indexable: true, blockers: [], faqs: [],
  headings: [
    { depth: 2, slug: 'why-this-site-exists', text: 'Why this site exists' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'meet-the-founder', text: 'Meet the founder' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'how-we-build-the-guides', text: 'How we build the guides' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'editorial-team', text: 'Editorial team and technical review' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'editorial-independence', text: 'Editorial independence' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'the-yousail-network', text: 'The YouSail network' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'contact-us', text: 'Contact us' },
  ],
  description: 'Meet the people behind Marine Diesel Engine Australia, learn why Gabriel Machuret created the site and see how its independent engine guides are researched.',
  primaryKeyword: 'about Marine Diesel Engine Australia', searchIntent: 'Navigational',
  extraSchema: [
    {
      '@type': 'Person',
      '@id': `${site.domain}/about/gabriel-machuret/#person`,
      name: 'Gabriel Machuret',
      url: `${site.domain}/about/gabriel-machuret/`,
      jobTitle: 'Founder',
      worksFor: { '@id': `${site.domain}/#publisher` },
      sameAs: ['https://yousail.com.au/'],
    },
  ],
};
---
<Page page={page}>
  <header class="about-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Independent Australian publication</p>
    <h1>About Marine Diesel Engine Australia</h1>
    <p>
      We make marine diesel information easier to find, compare and use. Marine
      Diesel Engine Australia is part of <a href="https://yousail.com.au/" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouSail</a>,
      an Australian sailing network created by Gabriel Machuret.
    </p>
    <div class="about-principles" aria-label="Our publishing principles">
      <span>Practical</span><span>Independent</span><span>Source-led</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <h2 id="why-this-site-exists">Why this site exists</h2>
  <p>
    Plan a life aboard a yacht and the engine stops being a mysterious box under
    the companionway. It becomes the system that gets you into an anchorage,
    charges essential equipment and brings you home when the wind disappears.
    Yet useful answers are scattered across manuals, manufacturer pages,
    workshop knowledge, forums and old owner discussions.
  </p>
  <p>
    We built this publication to organise that information around the decisions
    Australian boat owners actually make: understanding a symptom, planning a
    service, comparing engines, assessing a used boat, budgeting for a repower
    and finding the right professional help.
  </p>

  <section class="founder-card" aria-labelledby="meet-the-founder">
    <div class="founder-monogram" aria-hidden="true">GM</div>
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Founder</p>
      <h2 id="meet-the-founder">Meet Gabriel Machuret</h2>
      <p>
        Gabriel is a marketer, researcher and sailor in the making. He has spent
        more than a decade turning difficult-to-find information into useful
        digital resources. Marine Diesel Engine Australia applies that work to
        one of boating’s most technical and consequential subjects.
      </p>
      <a class="text-arrow" href="/about/gabriel-machuret/">Read Gabriel’s story <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2 id="how-we-build-the-guides">How we build the guides</h2>
  <p>
    Our starting point is primary evidence: current manufacturer specifications,
    engine manuals, Australian regulator guidance and recognised safety
    organisations. We compare claims across sources, state the model or duty
    context where it matters, link readers to the underlying material and mark
    the limits of general advice.
  </p>
  <div class="about-method-grid">
    <article>
      <strong>Start with the real question</strong>
      <span>What does a boat owner need to identify, compare or ask next?</span>
    </article>
    <article>
      <strong>Prefer primary sources</strong>
      <span>Manuals, manufacturer data and official Australian guidance come first.</span>
    </article>
    <article>
      <strong>Separate facts from judgement</strong>
      <span>Specifications are labelled; selection advice includes its assumptions.</span>
    </article>
    <article>
      <strong>Correct the record</strong>
      <span>Readers and businesses can submit evidence through our corrections process.</span>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p>
    Read our <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>, explore our
    <a href="/resources/">authority resources</a> or see how we handle
    <a href="/corrections/">corrections</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="editorial-team">Editorial team and technical review</h2>
  <p>
    “The Marine Diesel Engine editorial team” is our organisational publishing
    byline. It describes the research, writing, editing and maintenance of the
    site; it is not the name of a marine engineer and does not imply technical
    certification. Gabriel leads the publication and its information design.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a qualified person reviews a guide, that reviewer is identified by
    name on the page. If no reviewer is named, readers should not assume that a
    marine mechanic has certified the content.
  </p>

  <h2 id="editorial-independence">Editorial independence</h2>
  <p>
    We are an information publisher, not a marine workshop, engine distributor
    or parts seller. We do not diagnose individual engines or claim that a
    general guide replaces a model-specific manual, a qualified marine mechanic
    or an onboard inspection. A named technical reviewer appears only where a
    real person has reviewed that page.
  </p>
  <p>
    Business listings are not endorsements. Where a listing or referral is
    commercial, we disclose it. Safety-sensitive pages tell readers when to stop
    the engine and seek professional help. Before acting on a guide, read the
    <a href="/disclaimer/">information disclaimer</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-yousail-network">The YouSail network</h2>
  <p>
    Marine Diesel Engine Australia sits alongside a growing collection of
    practical Australian boating resources. The network covers marinas, sailing
    schools, courses, clubs, charters, sails and other parts of life on the water.
  </p>
  <div class="network-links" aria-label="YouSail network topics">
    <span>Top Marinas</span><span>Sailing Schools</span><span>Sailing Courses</span>
    <span>Sailing Clubs</span><span>Top Charters</span><span>Buy Sails</span>
  </div>

  <h2 id="contact-us">Contact us</h2>
  <p>
    If you represent a marine brand, maintain engines professionally, have a
    primary source we should review or have found a factual error, we would like
    to hear from you. Use our <a href="/contact/">contact form</a> or email
    <a href="mailto:gabriel@yousail.com.au">gabriel@yousail.com.au</a>.
  </p>
  <p class="about-close">
    For the wider Australian sailing project, visit
    <a href="https://yousail.com.au/" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouSail.com.au <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>.
  </p>
</Page>
